options:
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    author: W5TSU
    catch_exceptions: 'True'
    comment: 'Receives Thetis''s real over-the-air TX output (RADE V1 or FreeDV 700E, whichever the operator
      has armed via ZZDK/ZZEF and keyed) via HackRF, demodulates it as a standard USB/SSB signal (complex
      band-pass filter selecting the positive-frequency voice passband, then real part -- the standard
      software-defined way to do SSB demod when you have full I/Q, no phasing-method analog circuitry
      needed), and records the result to a mono wav. That wav is the input for an independent decode check:
      freedv-gui''s own reference decoder (''freedv -ut rx -utmode 700E'' or ''-utmode RADEV1'', see a
      local freedv-gui checkout) or Tools/FreeDV/make_fdv_test_iq.py''s own analysis conventions -- this
      flowgraph only captures and demodulates, it does not attempt to decode FreeDV/RADE itself. This
      is the missing half of the existing tx_700e_hackrf.grc / tx_radev1_hackrf.grc pair (which transmit
      a known-good signal *into* Thetis''s RX): this one listens to what Thetis''s TX chain actually puts
      out, closing the ''no off-air decode confirmation'' gap noted throughout FreeDV-Plan.md for both
      RADE V1 TX and FreeDV 700E TX.'
    copyright: ''
    description: Off-air capture of Thetis's own RADE V1 / FreeDV 700E TX output, via HackRF RX, for independent
      reference-decoder confirmation
    gen_linking: dynamic
    generate_options: qt_gui
    generator_class_name: PythonQtGuiGenerator
    generator_module: gnuradio.grc.workflows.python_qt_gui
    hier_block_src_path: '.:'
    id: rx_offair_capture_hackrf
    max_nouts: '0'
    output_language: python
    qt_qss_theme: ''
    realtime_scheduling: ''
    run: 'True'
    run_command: '{python} -u {filename}'
    thread_safe_setters: ''
    title: Thetis TX Off-Air Capture -- HackRF RX
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 10
    - 10
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
blocks:
- name: samp_rate
  id: variable
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF RX front-end rate. 2 Msps -- comfortably above the ~2 Msps floor the TX flowgraphs
      found HackRF needs for reliable quality (same hardware, same floor applies to RX), and a clean multiple
      of audio_rate (250:1) so the frequency-xlating filter's decimation is an exact integer.
    value: '2000000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 16
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: audio_rate
  id: variable
  parameters:
    comment: Output wav rate -- matches freedv_get_modem_sample_rate() for both 700E and RADE V1 (8 kHz)
      exactly, so the captured audio can be fed straight to freedv-gui's reference decoder with no rate
      ambiguity (unlike Tools/FreeDV/make_fdv_test_iq.py's --input-wav, which does handle arbitrary rates
      -- this file is for the reference-decoder path instead, which is pickier).
    value: '8000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 16
    - 76
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: center_freq
  id: parameter
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    hide: none
    comment: Must match whatever Thetis is actually transmitting on -- 14.236 MHz / DIGU is this project's
      established calling frequency for both RADE V1 and 700E testing (see Tools/thetis-ai-control's freedv-calling-frequencies
      table), same default the TX flowgraphs use. Change if Thetis is tuned elsewhere, but keep this and
      Thetis's dial frequency identical -- there is no frequency search here, just a fixed-tuned USB demod.
    label: Center Frequency (Hz)
    short_id: ''
    type: eng_float
    value: '14236000'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 180
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: rx_if_gain
  id: variable_qtgui_range
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF IF (VGA) gain. Thetis's real TX is a very strong, very close signal compared to typical
      off-air DX -- start LOW (this defaults to 8dB, well under the TX flowgraphs' 20dB) and raise only
      if the spectrum/waveform sink shows the signal is too weak; watch for a flat-topped/clipped time-domain
      trace as a sign this or rx_bb_gain is too high, which would corrupt the very thing being verified.
    gui_hint: 0,1,1,1
    label: RX IF/VGA Gain (dB)
    min_len: '200'
    orient: QtCore.Qt.Horizontal
    rangeType: int
    start: '0'
    step: '1'
    stop: '40'
    value: '8'
    widget: counter_slider
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 400
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: rx_bb_gain
  id: variable_qtgui_range
  parameters:
    comment: HackRF baseband gain -- same start-low guidance as rx_if_gain above.
    gui_hint: 0,2,1,1
    label: RX BB Gain (dB)
    min_len: '200'
    orient: QtCore.Qt.Horizontal
    rangeType: int
    start: '0'
    step: '1'
    stop: '40'
    value: '8'
    widget: counter_slider
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 700
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: out_file
  id: parameter
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    hide: none
    comment: Captured mono USB audio, 8 kHz float32 wav. Rename per capture (this does NOT auto-timestamp)
      so successive test runs don't overwrite each other -- e.g. offair_tx_radev1_<date>.wav vs offair_tx_700e_<date>.wav,
      matching this repo's convention of a descriptive name over a bare default (see offair_14236000_RADEV1_20260808.wav).
    label: Output wav (captured demodulated audio)
    short_id: ''
    type: str
    value: '"offair_capture.wav"'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 1000
    - 12
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
