- name: freq_xlating_fir_filter_xxx_0
  id: freq_xlating_fir_filter_xxx
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: 'USB demod + decimate to audio_rate in one block: a complex band-pass covering only +300..+2700
      Hz (the positive-frequency side -- USB) rejects the mirror-image LSB content that a real-valued
      filter couldn''t distinguish. center_freq=0 since osmosdr_source is already tuned exactly to center_freq
      (the dial frequency), so no further shift is needed here, only filtering + decimation.'
    center_freq: '0'
    decim: samp_rate // audio_rate
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    samp_rate: samp_rate
    taps: firdes.complex_band_pass(1, samp_rate, 300, 2700, 300)
    type: ccc
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 500
    - 220
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: blocks_complex_to_real_0
  id: blocks_complex_to_real
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: Taking the real part after a one-sided (positive-frequency-only) complex band-pass IS how
      software SSB/USB demod works with full I/Q -- no separate phasing-method mixer stage needed.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    vlen: '1'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 800
    - 220
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: blocks_wavfile_sink_0
  id: blocks_wavfile_sink
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    append: 'False'
    comment: Runs until you press GRC's stop button (unlike the TX flowgraphs, which play a fixed-length
      wav once and stop automatically -- there's no natural end-of-signal marker on receive, so start
      this flowgraph, key Thetis's TX per the usual thetisctl --confirm-tx protocol, let the transmission
      run, then stop this once Thetis unkeys).
    file: out_file
    nchan: '1'
    samp_rate: audio_rate
    format: FORMAT_WAV
    bits_per_sample1: FORMAT_PCM_16
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 1000
    - 220
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: qtgui_freq_sink_x_0
  id: qtgui_freq_sink_x
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    alpha1: '1.0'
    alpha2: '1.0'
    alpha3: '1.0'
    alpha4: '1.0'
    alpha5: '1.0'
    alpha6: '1.0'
    alpha7: '1.0'
    alpha8: '1.0'
    alpha9: '1.0'
    alpha10: '1.0'
    autoscale: 'False'
    average: '0.2'
    axislabels: 'True'
    bw: samp_rate
    color1: '"blue"'
    color10: '"dark blue"'
    color2: '"red"'
    color3: '"green"'
    color4: '"black"'
    color5: '"cyan"'
    color6: '"magenta"'
    color7: '"yellow"'
    color8: '"dark red"'
    color9: '"dark green"'
    comment: Wide-view spectrum at the HackRF's native rate, before the USB filter -- confirms the signal
      is actually present and roughly centred before trusting the demodulated audio. Expect 700E's clean
      OFDM comb or RADE V1's broadband/noise-like signature, matching the TX flowgraphs' own spectrum-sink
      comments.
    ctrlpanel: 'False'
    fc: center_freq
    fftsize: '1024'
    freqhalf: 'True'
    grid: 'False'
    gui_hint: ''
    label: Relative Gain
    label1: ''
    label2: ''''''
    label3: ''''''
    label4: ''''''
    label5: ''''''
    label6: ''''''
    label7: ''''''
    label8: ''''''
    label9: ''''''
    label10: ''''''
    legend: 'True'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    name: '"RX spectrum (wideband, pre-demod)"'
    nconnections: '1'
    norm_window: 'False'
    showports: 'False'
    tr_chan: '0'
    tr_level: '0.0'
    tr_mode: qtgui.TRIG_MODE_FREE
    tr_tag: '""'
    type: complex
    units: dB
    update_time: '0.10'
    width1: '1'
    width2: '1'
    width3: '1'
    width4: '1'
    width5: '1'
    width6: '1'
    width7: '1'
    width8: '1'
    width9: '1'
    width10: '1'
    wintype: window.WIN_BLACKMAN_hARRIS
    ymax: '10'
    ymin: '-140'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate:
    - 500
    - 60
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: qtgui_time_sink_x_0
  id: qtgui_time_sink_x
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    alpha1: '1.0'
    alpha2: '1.0'
    alpha3: '1.0'
    alpha4: '1.0'
    alpha5: '1.0'
    alpha6: '1.0'
    alpha7: '1.0'
    alpha8: '1.0'
    alpha9: '1.0'
    alpha10: '1.0'
    autoscale: 'False'
    axislabels: 'True'
    color1: blue
    color2: '"red"'
    color3: '"green"'
    color4: '"black"'
    color5: '"cyan"'
    color6: '"magenta"'
    color7: '"yellow"'
    color8: '"dark red"'
    color9: '"dark green"'
    color10: '"dark blue"'
    comment: Demodulated audio waveform after the USB filter -- watch for a flat-topped/clipped trace
      as a sign rx_if_gain/rx_bb_gain need lowering (this is a very strong, very close signal, not a weak
      off-air catch), and for real varying content (not a flat line) as basic confirmation something is
      actually being received.
    ctrlpanel: 'False'
    entags: 'True'
    grid: 'False'
    gui_hint: ''
    label1: ''''''
    label2: ''''''
    label3: ''''''
    label4: ''''''
    label5: ''''''
    label6: ''''''
    label7: ''''''
    label8: ''''''
    label9: ''''''
    label10: ''''''
    legend: 'True'
    marker1: '-1'
    marker2: '-1'
    marker3: '-1'
    marker4: '-1'
    marker5: '-1'
    marker6: '-1'
    marker7: '-1'
    marker8: '-1'
    marker9: '-1'
    marker10: '-1'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    name: '"Demodulated audio"'
    nconnections: '1'
    size: audio_rate
    srate: audio_rate
    stemplot: 'False'
    style1: '1'
    style2: '1'
    style3: '1'
    style4: '1'
    style5: '1'
    style6: '1'
    style7: '1'
    style8: '1'
    style9: '1'
    style10: '1'
    tr_chan: '0'
    tr_delay: '0'
    tr_level: '0.0'
    tr_mode: qtgui.TRIG_MODE_FREE
    tr_slope: qtgui.TRIG_SLOPE_POS
    tr_tag: '""'
    type: float
    update_time: '0.10'
    width1: '1'
    width2: '1'
    width3: '1'
    width4: '1'
